        "title": "Two-Stage Active—Passive Synergistic Water Depth Estimation Using Shallow Water Probability Constraint and Multi-Feature Attention ResNet",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3736576",
        "authors": "Qianqian Wang, Zepeng Wang, Fangfang Zhang, Jinbo An, Shenglei Wang, Yongqing Chen, Junsheng Li",
        "abstract": "The synergy of active and passive satellite remote sensing has emerged as a mainstream methodology for bathymetric estimation. Nevertheless, traditional models struggle with limited in-situ samples, weak signals in deep-water areas, and spatial heterogeneity of benthic substrates. This study develops a two-stage deep learning framework integrating “optical shallow-water identification” and “bathymetric estimation.” An attention-based U-Net variant is first utilized to achieve automated recognition of shallow-water areas and extract a probability map as a spatial constraint feature. This is followed by the development of the SSF-HAResNet model, which fuses these spatial priors with raw spectral data, dual-band ratios, Depth Invariant Indices (DII), and textural features. The framework was evaluated using a comprehensive dataset of 64 reefs in the South China Sea (Dongsha, Xisha, Zhongsha, and Nansha). Experimental results indicate that the coordination of multi-dimensional features significantly strengthens the model's feature expression in deeper waters. Based on depth-stratified accuracy assessment and cross-regional validation, the proposed framework provides reliable operational bathymetric mapping capability to approximately 35 m under clear-water reef conditions. Across 13 independent test sites encompassing diverse coral reef morphologies and varying acquisition dates, the model consistently achieved an R^{2} of 0.95 and an RMSE of 1.74 m. These results indicate good spatio-temporal transferability and demonstrate the model’ s ability to capture bathymetric patterns under varying environmental and multi-temporal imaging conditions. The study validates that integrating physical spectral indices and spatial constraints can effectively alleviate the impact of substrate heterogeneity, offering a reliable technical foundation for large-scale bathymetric surveys.",
