        "title": "Residual Multiple Suppression Using a Physics-Informed Plug-in Network With Residual-Multiple-Level Prior",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3736843",
        "authors": "Zhiyu Yao, Wenkai Lu, Weiheng Geng, Jinpeng Liu, Mingrui Zhong, Yili Ren",
        "abstract": "Multiple suppression is a fundamental problem in seismic processing because residual multiples often exhibit kinematic characteristics similar to those of primary reflections, making these interfering events hard to remove cleanly in practice. As a result, conventional methods usually face a trade-off: aggressive parameter settings tend to damage useful primaries, whereas conservative settings often leave residual multiple energy insufficiently attenuated. To address this issue, this article presents a physics-informed plug-in network for residual multiple suppression. Instead of replacing conventional demultiple pipelines, the proposed method takes preliminary demultiple results as input and performs targeted refinement on the remaining residual multiples. The framework first estimates a physics-guided residual-multiple-level prior to characterize the spatial distribution of residual contamination. This prior then guides an attention-based attenuation network to apply spatially varying suppression strength. In addition, an optional geological-structure-guided recovery step can further return primary-consistent energy from the removed component. For training, samples are adaptively synthesized from real-data-derived primary and residual-multiple proxies. In this way, the method preserves compatibility with existing industrial workflows while improving the balance between residual attenuation and primary preservation. Experiments on synthetic and field data show that the proposed method suppresses residual multiple energy more effectively than conventional baselines while better preserving the continuity and amplitude consistency of primary reflections.",
