        "authors": "Daniel Lopez, Ralf Bennartz, Kristen E. Fauria",
        "abstract": "We explore the potential of Passive Microwave (PMW) sensors for analyzing volcanic ash plumes from explosive volcanic eruptions. Utilizing data from the Global Precipitation Measurement (GPM) Microwave Imager (GMI) and the Special Sensor Microwave Imager/Sounder (SSMIS), we conducted an examination of three volcanic eruptions with a Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI) greater than four. These eruptions included the 2021-2022 Hunga Volcano, 2021 Fukutoku-Oka-no-Ba, and the 2019 Raikoke eruptions. Our methodology involved calculating the difference between observed and simulated clear-sky brightness temperatures to isolate the volcanic signal from the background. The simulated background brightness temperatures were derived from radiative transfer simulations, which model the transfer of electromagnetic radiation through the atmosphere, based on ERA-5 reanalysis data. Our findings indicate that PMW sensors can differentiate signals of volcanic eruptions from other meteorological processes. For low frequency channels, we typically see a difference of brightness temperature with a magnitude less than 10K, while high frequency channels have a brightness temperature magnitude change of -75K. Notably, volcanic plumes exhibit a unique signature and are easily distinguishable from meteorological convection based on the 37H and the 150H channels. As PMW observations are insensitive to small ice particles, the technique proposed here provides information on plumes also under conditions where ice clouds limit the usability of traditional infrared and visible sensors.",
