        "title": "From Noisy Post-Stack Profiles to Reflectivity: Two-Stage Complementary Multi-Task Network",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3733825",
        "authors": "Ning Wu, Haiyang Xu, Yue Li, Yanan Tian, Qiyu Wang",
        "abstract": "High-resolution seismic profiles, particularly reflectivity, are crucial for accurately delineating subsurface thin layers, faults, and fine-scale stratigraphy. However, when seismic sections are affected by both low resolution and noise interference, super-resolution techniques can stably enhance spatial resolution under noisy conditions, but they often fail to achieve the frequency resolution required for identifying complex geological features. In contrast, deconvolution methods are theoretically capable of recovering reflectivity with high frequency resolution, but their sensitivity to noise often results in unreliable reflectivity estimates. To address these limitations, we propose a sequential two-stage high-resolution processing framework that leverages the complementary strengths of super-resolution and seismic deconvolution techniques. In the first stage, we designed a multi-task seismic super-resolution network (MSRNet) to generate noise-free, high spatial resolution profiles, which serve as prior information for the subsequent deconvolution process. In the second stage, we designed a multi-task seismic deconvolution network (MSDNet) with multi-stream information fusion capability to derive the seismic reflectivity with both high spatial and frequency resolution, enabling detailed characterization of subsurface structures. To enhance the network's robustness to noise and preserve weak reflection signals, we incorporated an Enhanced Denoising Module (EDM) and a Multi-Scale Stratigraphic Feature Fusion Module (MFF) into both stages of the framework. Additionally, to suppress false reflections and obtain a reliable approximate solution, we adopt a multitask architecture incorporates a structural constraint loss and a positional constraint loss to guide the network in accurately localizing reflection axes. Furthermore, a convolutional reconstruction loss is introduced to ensure the reversibility between reflectivity and seismic profiles. Synthetic examples demonstrate that the proposed two-stage network effectively preserves weak signals and accurately predicts the reflectivity of complex subsurface structures, even in the presence of random and coherent noise. Field examples further validate the practical applicability of the proposed method, revealing reasonable consistency with the reflectivity log.",
