        "abstract": "Accurate modeling of electromagnetic with induced polarization (EM–IP) effects in semi-airborne time-domain electromagnetic (SATEM) systems is essential for detecting polarizable targets. Conventional studies focus mainly on Off-time stage responses, leaving On-time stage behavior insufficiently explored. This study develops a 3-D full-waveform EM–IP modeling approach by integrating the finite volume method (FVM) with an adaptive sum-of-exponentials (ASOE) approximation. By dynamically adjusting the number of exponential terms with time-step size, ASOE approximation improves the efficiency of Caputo fractional-derivative evaluation compared with the SOE approach. The ASOE-FVM method is validated against 1-D analytical and 3-D numerical solutions. Results reveal that full-waveform EM-IP responses driven by trapezoidal current waveform, exhibit sign reversals in both Steady stage and Off-time stages. Analysis of the trapezoidal source waveform parameters shows the turn-on and turn-off durations primarily control early-time amplitudes in Steady stage and Off-time stage, respectively, whereas the Steady stage duration, together with electrical properties of the anomalous body, governs temporal occurrence of sign reversal in Off-time stage response. This indicates that Steady stage data provide more stable constraints on polarization characteristics. Further analysis shows that longer transmitters, full-waveform acquisition, lower receiver altitudes, and optimized survey layouts enhance detection resolution for polarizable anomalies. Finally, field observation experiments using an EM–IP equivalent coil further verify the accuracy of the full-waveform numerical simulations and the effectiveness of full-waveform observation.",
