        "abstract": "Full-Waveform Inversion (FWI) is a key seismic imaging method, posed as a nonlinear optimization problem to recover high-resolution subsurface models. Its usual least-squares (L^{2}) misfit, however, often converges to local minima because of the objective’s strong non-convexity and cycle skipping. To mitigate this, recent work explores Optimal Transport (OT)-based misfits, particularly the squared Wasserstein-2 distance (W^{2}_{2}), which has better convexity properties. This paper analytically characterizes this metric by deriving a simplified one-dimensional (1D) expression for the gradient of W^{2}_{2} under assumptions relevant to early-stage FWI. This enables a direct quantitative comparison between W^{2}_{2} and L^{2} gradients and clarifies the theoretical advantages of the Wasserstein metric for estimating acoustic velocity in homogeneous regions, improving FWI robustness and efficiency.",
