        "title": "An interaction-aware framework for ICESat-2 elevation control point extraction using photon-level distribution metrics and joint threshold optimization",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3736574",
        "authors": "Xiaoxiao Zhu, Sheng Nie, Zhikun Ren, Cheng Wang, Xiaohuan Xi, Jinrui Liu, Haomin Ji, Guanghao Ha",
        "abstract": "The spaceborne photon-counting LiDAR mission ICESat-2 provides a unique opportunity to generate globally distributed elevation control points (ECPs) with high vertical accuracy. Most existing ICESat-2 based ECP extraction methods rely on empirically defined thresholds applied to ATL08 parameters and treat filtering variables independently, which limits robustness and transferability under heterogeneous terrain and noisy photon conditions. In this study, we propose an interaction-aware framework for ICESat-2 ECP extraction that jointly exploits ATL03 photon-level information and ATL08 segment-level metrics. Two novel distribution-aware photon-level parameters are introduced to characterize the vertical clustering properties of terrain-corrected ground photons. A systematic parameter screening strategy combining Spearman correlation analysis and quantitative threshold-response evaluation is used to identify key variables controlling ECP accuracy. Based on the selected parameters, a grid-search-based joint threshold optimization scheme is implemented to explicitly account for parameter interactions and to derive optimal threshold combinations. Results demonstrate that the proposed framework substantially improves both the accuracy and reliability of ICESat-2 ECPs. Across flat, hilly, and mountainous terrain, root mean square error (RMSE) is reduced from 1.69 m, 3.35 m, and 6.00 m to 0.28 m, 0.35 m, and 0.68 m, respectively, while the accuracy-passing ratio (APR) increases from 84.00%, 68.54%, and 48.50% to 93.43%, 95.42%, and 96.30%. The inclusion of the newly proposed photon-level parameters provides additional but consistent gains, yielding further RMSE reductions of up to 0.22 m and APR increases of up to 4.40%, particularly in complex terrain. Compared with conventional multi-parameter threshold superposition and a representative state-of-the-art method, the proposed interaction-aware optimization consistently achieves more favorable trade-offs among elevation accuracy, completeness, and reliability. Overall, this study provides a robust and scalable methodology for constructing high-quality ICESat-2 ECP datasets, supporting large-scale topographic mapping and multi-sensor geometric calibration.",
