        "title": "Transient Matching Extracting Transform and Its Application in Deep Carbonate Reflection Structure Identification",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3736621",
        "authors": "Peng Chen, Xiuquan Hu, Hui Chen, Ying Hu, Yutao Xie",
        "abstract": "Characterization of reflection structures in deep carbonate strata is crucial for identifying sedimentary sequence boundaries and predicting effective reservoirs in seismic interpretation. However, constrained by the strong frequency variation and multi-mode aliasing inherent in deep signals, conventional time-frequency (TF) analysis methods struggle to finely and accurately characterize reflection structures. To address this issue, this study proposes a Transient Matching Extracting Transform (TMET) to more effectively characterize stratal reflection structures. First, within the short-time Fourier transform domain, the method exploits the differential mathematical relationship between the instantaneous frequency estimator and the group delay (GD) estimator to construct a matched frequency-varying rate estimator (MS_{GD}), thereby precisely acquiring the frequency-varying characteristics of each mode. Subsequently, based on the properties of the MS_{GD}, a frequency-varying rate valid domain (S_{GD}VD) is defined to constrain the computational interval of the MS_{GD}. Next, within the S_{GD}VD of each mode, a frequency-directional demodulation transform is performed on the corresponding MS_{GD}, and a peak criterion is applied to the resulting output to extract the GD trajectories of individual modes. Finally, the corresponding TF coefficients are extracted along the retrieved GD trajectories, which enhances TF energy concentration while preserving signal reconstruction precision. Numerical experiments have validated this property. Applications to a synthetic geological model and field deep carbonate seismic data demonstrate that the proposed TMET serves as a potentially effective tool for the identification of stratal reflection structures.",
