        "abstract": "Tropospheric delays constitute the principal error source in the inversion of surface deformation using Interferometric synthetic aperture radar (InSAR) techniques. Here, a novel method is proposed to estimate the tropospheric delays and improve the accuracy of deformation inversion. First, a small baseline subset (SBAS) is divided into several common-reference interferometric subsets (CRISs). Subsequently, the CRISs optimally selected by a lookup table were employed to construct an inversion model based on the spatiotemporal distribution characteristics of tropospheric delays. The lookup table was constructed through 540 000 simulation tests. Finally, the estimated tropospheric delays are applied to correct the interferograms, enabling high-accuracy deformation inversion. The performance of the new method was validated through simulated and real tests. The simulation results indicate that the new method accurately estimates tropospheric delays with an average RMSE of 2.4 mm while achieving reductions of 56% and 62% in deformation RMSE compared to two commonly used methods. A real-world validation in Southern California confirms that the new method achieves an improvement of 29%–69% in deformation accuracy over four commonly used methods. Furthermore, validation on the Qinghai–Tibet Plateau demonstrates that the new strategy can correct the tropospheric delays of co-seismic interferograms with small-to-moderate magnitude earthquakes, enabling the accurate extraction of centimeter- to subcentimeter-scale deformation fields.",
