        "abstract": "The elastic and anisotropic parameters of shale reservoirs with sparsely distributed vertical fractures cannot be decoded accurately from seismic data with classical deep learning seismic inversion methods for insufficient well-logging data. To fill this gap, we derive a novel quadratic approximate PP-Wave reflection coefficient equation for shale reservoirs with sparsely distributed vertical fractures and propose a physics-embedded deep learning inversion method of anisotropy parameters by embedding the novel equation into the deep learning models. Specifically, we derive a novel quadratic approximate PP-Wave reflection coefficient equation, which corrects the error caused by treating the average P-wave phase angle as the incident P-wave phase angle. In addition, we construct a series of intermediate parameters and rewrite the derived reflection coefficients in the form of the incident angle representation. The new form can be embedded into the stepwise deep learning inversion workflow to improve the stability and generalization. Finally, a physics-embedded deep learning inversion method is proposed by coupling deep learning models, the novel equation, and the seismic convolution model, which alleviates the problem of poor generalization and stability caused by insufficient training samples. The field data example indicates that the proposed method can be successfully applied to the inversion task of the fractured shale oil and gas survey.",
