        "abstract": "Seismic waves experience amplitude attenuation and phase distortion during propagation in absorptive media. These effects reduce the resolution of seismic data. The absorption attenuation is especially strong in shallow layers. These loose and viscoelastic materials degrade data quality. Recent deep learning methods can map attenuation data to stationary data. However, they ignore the dominant role of shallow attenuation. We analyze the relationship between shallow attenuation and overall attenuation. Based on the analysis, we propose the concept of a shallow-dominant Q field. We find that a single-layer Q field fits the exponential attenuation framework better. Therefore, an adaptive exponential module is proposed to generate shallow-dominant attenuation data through the network. We propose a new multi-modal attenuation compensation method by combining shallow-dominant attenuation collaborative mapping. This method uses a decoupled process. We first model the shallow-dominant attenuation data. Then, we perform compensation mapping together with the attenuation data to achieve higher precision. We design a weighted loss function combining label supervision and data reconstruction. This loss can reduce learning difficulty and improve compensation performance. Tests on both synthetic and field data show that the proposed method effectively restores weak deep signals and significantly enhances attenuation compensation results.",
