        "abstract": "Existing deep learning-based full waveform inversion (DL-FWI) methods offer an end-to-end solution; however, they do not explicitly model the time–depth relationship inherent in conventional seismic processing (CSP) and lack a low-frequency-guided reconstruction strategy used in conventional full waveform inversion (FWI), leading to limited resolution in deep formations. To address these issues, we propose time-to-depth mapping fusion FWI (TDMF-FWI), a physics-inspired dual-branch FWI framework that achieves structurally guided detail enhancement by decoupling global background estimation from local detail recovery via learnable time-to-depth (TD) mapping modules. The low-frequency learnable time-to-depth (L-T2D) mapping branch leverages a Gaussian-biased cross-domain window attention (Gauss-CDWA) mechanism to construct a structurally consistent low-frequency velocity prior. A window reorganization strategy, together with relative positional encoding, enforces time–depth consistency by bridging structural correspondence across domains. The high-frequency learnable time-to-depth (H-T2D) mapping branch extracts temporal features, learns contextual representations, and enhances spatial coherence to enable recovery of fine-scale geological details. A learnable nonlinear resampling module performs data-driven time—depth mapping. Moreover, the low-frequency affine modulation (LAM) module generates affine parameters from low-frequency features and adaptively modulates high-frequency features, thereby jointly optimizing global structural consistency and local detail fidelity. Our method achieves superior performance compared to state-of-the-art data-driven approaches on synthetic datasets (OpenFWI, SEG simulation, and Marmousi II slice), especially in reconstructing sharp boundaries and deep complex structures, and delivers promising performance on the real-world FAN-10000m benchmark. The source code is available at https://github.com/FanSmale/TDMF-FWI"