        "abstract": "The massive memory requirement of source wave-field reconstruction (SWR) in GPU-accelerated 3D elastic reverse time migration (ERTM) severely limits practical large-scale geological imaging. To address this bottleneck, this study proposes a boundary wavefield compression method integrating the single-layer boundary saving method (S-BSM), Nyquist sampling, and a CUDA-accelerated Low-Rank Adaptive Compressed Singular Value Decomposition (LoRA-cSVD) algorithm. Three key innovations are introduced: (1) Combining S-BSM with Nyquist sampling (NS-BSM) to generate lightweight 2D single-layer boundary wavefield data for cSVD compression; (2) Using the singular value matrix of cSVD to adaptively determine the compression rank under a predefined energy loss ratio; (3) Dynamically adjusting the calculation rank of cSVD based on the compression rank from the previous time step. Numerical results demonstrate that LoRA-cSVD compresses boundary wavefield data by approximately 10-fold to the megabyte scale versus NS-BSM, with a mere approximately 6% increase in computational time compared to standalone forward simulation. The compressed boundary wavefield data can fully reside in VRAM, eliminating redundant memory copying and outperforming S-BSM by approximately 7% in overall computational efficiency. This study provides a practical, memory-efficient solution for large-scale 3D geological imaging while maintaining high imaging accuracy.",
