        "title": "Efficient Three-Dimensional Electroseismic Modeling Based on CNCSU-FDTD and Wavefield Decomposition",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3723712",
        "authors": "Xinyuan Li, Xuejiao Zhao, Li Han, Yibing Yu, Yanju Ji",
        "abstract": "As the reciprocal counterpart of the seismoelectric effect, the electroseismic effect holds potential for delineating elastic and electrical interfaces, and numerical simulation is crucial for investigating its coupling mechanisms. Compared with widely used 2-D methods, 3-D electroseismic modeling can capture realistic wave propagation in exploration scenarios, but the requisite dense spatial sampling and long simulation time windows render the electromagnetic part of the modeling computationally prohibitive. To address these issues, we propose a 3-D electroseismic modeling method that combines the Crank–Nicolson–cycle–sweep–uniform finite-difference time-domain (CNCSU-FDTD) scheme with the background–anomaly wavefield decomposition. By neglecting the weak electrofiltration feedback, the proposed method decouples the governing equations and enables sequential solutions of the electromagnetic (EM) and seismic fields. For the EM field, the CNCSU-FDTD scheme efficiently solves the large-scale operator matrix equations within the Crank–Nicolson (CN) formulation and relaxes the EM time-step constraints. The resulting electric field is then used as a source term to solve the seismic wavefield using a high-order finite-difference scheme. Additionally, the proposed method decomposes the total electroseismic wavefield into background and anomalous components induced by the background and anomalous EM fields, respectively, thereby enabling investigation of the generation mechanisms of electroseismic interface responses. The efficiency and accuracy of the proposed method are verified using full-space and half-space models. In a hydrocarbon reservoir anomaly model, wavefield decomposition is used to investigate the 3-D characteristics of the electroseismic interface response and reveal its generation mechanisms. Results show that the P-wave component of the interface response arises from two mechanisms: an equivalent stress source associated with elastic contrasts (elastic mechanism) and an equivalent current source induced by conductivity contrasts (electric mechanism). This method provides a theoretical basis for studying electroseismic wavefield propagation and interface conversion.",
