        "abstract": "Traditional numerical seismic simulation methods generate staircase approximation artifacts and severe numerical dispersion when simulating complex geological interfaces, while their parallel computing performance is also restricted. To overcome these drawbacks, this work develops an elastic multi-relaxation-time lattice Boltzmann model (EM-LBM) for solid seismic wavefield forward modeling. We reconstruct the equilibrium distribution function, governing conservation equations and moment relaxation rules suitable for elastic media. The macroscopic elastic wave equation is strictly recovered via Chapman–Enskog multi-scale expansion, forming a physically sound theoretical link between mesoscopic particle kinetics and macroscopic continuum mechanics. Three numerical experiments, including homogeneous medium, layered medium and undulating heterogeneous seabed models, are designed to systematically verify the performance of the proposed EM-LBM. We compare the simulated wavefields, waveform profiles, common-shot gathers, single seismic traces and frequency spectra with high-order finite difference (FDM) benchmark results. Numerical tests indicate that the EM-LBM can precisely reproduce the propagation, reflection, transmission and mode conversion of P-and S-waves. It effectively mitigates numerical dispersion and artificial energy loss, and maintains favorable stability and energy preservation for rugged, highly heterogeneous formations. Benefiting from the clear physical meaning of mesoscopic algorithms and the natural parallelism of lattice discretization, the proposed model provides a stable, high-precision and interface-friendly mesoscopic simulation tool, which can act as an effective complement to conventional macroscopic numerical approaches for complicated geophysical modeling.",
