        "authors": "Siwei Zhu, Jianhui Li, Xiangyun Hu",
        "abstract": "Once dismissed as noise in geophysical exploration, 50/60 Hz electromagnetic (EM) fields from overhead AC transmission lines are now recognized as potential sources for subsur-face characterization. Yet prior work remains largely qualitative and quantitative inversion has yet to be attempted—partly due to realistic line-modeling challenges and unknown operational currents. To evaluate inversion feasibility, we develop one- and three-dimensional forward-modeling algorithms incorporating practical transmission-line parameters and sagging geometry, and conduct comprehensive simulations. Two key findings emerge: (1) A primary-field-dominated zone beneath the lines, where line currents govern the magnetic field, suggests that magnetic data collected there can accurately estimate transmission-line current parameters, as validated by NSGA-II optimization of synthetic data. (2) Outside this zone, transmission-line-induced EM fields, especially electric components, are strongly sensitive to subsurface conductive anomalies, with higher-order harmonics improving spatial resolution and depth sensitivity. These results confirm the viability of transmission-line EM fields for quantitative subsurface imaging and supply essential modeling tools and numerical justification for future inversion.",
