        "abstract": "The semi-airborne transient electromagnetic (SATEM) 3D inversion is computationally intensive and time-consuming. Multi-GPU parallelization is a promising approach to improve the inversion efficiency. However, when using multiple GPUs, failing to account for the actual workload assigned to each GPU not only leads to significant disparities in GPU utilization but also affects the inversion efficiency. To address these issues, we propose a multi-GPU parallel computing framework for the Gauss-Newton SATEM inversion that jointly optimizes inter-GPU and intra-GPU computation. At the scheduling level across multi-GPUs, we propose an auto-tuning load-balanced scheduling strategy based on the longest processing time (LPT) priority rule, which assigns computational tasks to GPUs according to estimated weights for improving hardware utilization across multiple GPU devices. At the computational level on each GPU, we introduce two schemes to solve the adjoint forward equations for the Jacobian computation, including a task-partitioned cuThomasBatch implementation and a pre-factorization ThomasBatch implementation that reuses matrix decomposition across multiple right-hand sides. Furthermore, we propose an adaptive solver selection mechanism that provides a practical guideline for choosing the optimal algorithm based on the problem scale, defined by the number of model grids and observed data. Finally, numerical experiments on synthetic and field datasets show that the proposed framework significantly improves sensitivity calculation and inversion efficiency.",
