        "title": "Local Frequency-Guided Peak Extracting Transform for Reflection Interface Identification in Deep Carbonate Reservoirs",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3725493",
        "authors": "Yasong Zhao, Xuping Chen, Hui Chen, Ying Hu",
        "abstract": "Seismic reflection signals from deep carbonate reservoirs exhibit strong non-stationarity, multi-scale superposition, and interference among closely spaced reflection interfaces. These characteristics often cause TF energy dispersion and blurred reflection-interface responses, limiting the accurate identification of complex subsurface structures. To address this issue, this study proposes a Local Frequency-guided Peak Extracting Transform (LFPET). First, a total variation regularization model is employed to estimate the instantaneous frequency, and the Split Bregman algorithm is adopted to obtain a smooth, stable, and noise-robust estimate of the local frequency. The estimated local frequency is then incorporated into the normalized W transform (NWT) framework to replace the time-varying dominant frequency, enabling adaptive TF energy concentration and enhanced noise robustness. Finally, a peak-driven coefficient redistribution strategy is introduced, in which TF coefficients are reassigned toward local energy peaks identified along the time direction to better localize signal energy. Experiments on synthetic signals, geological models, and field seismic data demonstrate that LFPET significantly improves TF energy concentration, effectively resolves closely spaced reflection interfaces, and exhibits strong robustness to noise. These advantages facilitate more accurate characterization of complex reflection interfaces, reservoir boundaries, and subsurface structures in deep carbonate reservoirs.",
