        "abstract": "Horizontal transversely isotropic (HTI) media can be used to characterize vertical or high-angle fractures developed in isotropic backgrounds and are widely applied in unconventional oil and gas exploration. Accurately inverting their anisotropic parameters is of great significance for fracture prediction and fluid identification. However, due to the introduction of the azimuthal dimension and the low contribution of anisotropic parameters to seismic responses, inversions that rely solely on azimuthal seismic data often suffer from severe ill-posedness, with stability highly dependent on effective prior constraints. To address this issue, this paper integrates fracture distribution characteristics induced by faults and folds, and combines multiple geometric attributes to explore their potential as prior information for fracture parameters. A fast generalized structurally constrained pre-stack seismic inversion (FGSCI) method for anisotropic parameters is accordingly proposed. First, based on 3D coordinate rotation transformations, we systematically derive intrinsic coordinate systems for various geological structures in a left-handed coordinate system. Second, combining 3D coordinate translation, we construct geometric attribute-constrained operators to achieve precise quantification of fracture density. Finally, by integrating these coordinate transformations, we build generalized structurally constrained operators and embed them into a 3D multi-trace inversion framework through matrix operations, such as the n-mode product, and design a constrained objective function and its fast optimization algorithm. Numerical experiments and practical applications demonstrate that this method exhibits strong stability, spatial fidelity and resolution.",
