        "title": "Cenozoic Post‐Collisional Au‐Rich Porphyry System Imaged by a High‐Density Seismic Array and Magma Records in the SE Margin of the Tibetan Plateau",
        "doi": "10.1029/2025jb033663",
        "authors": "Jue Hou, Guiping Yu, Bo Xu, Yingjie Yang, Xiaobo Tian, Tianyang Gao, Maowen Yuan, Zhuang Miao, Lei Gao, Nian Yu",
        "abstract": "Abstract Post‐collisional porphyry Cu‐Au deposits are prime targets for modern exploration, yet the source and intra‐crustal evolution of the associated magma remain poorly constrained. Here, we image the fine structure of the ∼45 km thick cratonic crust underlying the Beiya Au‐Cu deposit, an Eocene post‐collisional porphyry ore system, using a 268 km long dense short‐period seismic array across the eastern Tibetan plateau. Our receiver function image reveals, for the first time, two groups of positive seismic features with high Vp/Vs ratios in the lower crust. Integrated with published joint Vs inversion image, lower crustal xenolith records, and Sr‐Nd isotope simulation, our results indicate that the lower crust was locally modified by previous subduction and later melt to generate the Eocene fertile magma during collision. Ambient noise tomography further reveals three low‐Vs bands, extending obliquely upward from 8 to 2 km depth, spatially coinciding with collision‐inducted strike‐slip faults. Vertical Vs gradients identify two distinct centers in each low‐velocity band, located at ∼6 km and at <2 km depth, respectively. These observations, together with P‐T estimation of crystallized minerals from Eocene porphyries, indicate that the fertile magma evolved within a chamber at ∼6 km depth and then ascended upward along a strike‐slip fault into the upper crust, where it accumulated in shallow cupolas at depths of 1–2 km and ultimately formed the Beiya deposit. We propose that a closed magma chamber with a volume >100 km 3 in the shallow crust is a key factor controlling the formation of such deposits. Our study provides a typical example for understanding the intra‐crustal magma system associated with post‐collisional porphyry deposits.",
