        "abstract": "SUMMARY The Hellenic Arc Subduction Zone is the most seismically active region in the Mediterranean, capable of generating large earthquakes and tsunami. Given the proximity of densely populated coastlines, understanding the characteristics of potential future large tsunamigenic earthquakes is crucial for assessing tsunami hazard. We present nonlinear shallow water tsunami simulations sourced from the static seafloor displacements of five $M_{\\rm W}$ 8–9 dynamic rupture earthquake scenarios along the Hellenic Arc, varying in hypocentral location, rupture extent and moment magnitude. Our results show that shallow slip and off-fault plastic deformation control the vertical near-trench uplift, tsunami height, and tsunami arrival time, particularly towards the south-west of the Hellenic Arc. Plasticity increases the tsunami amplitudes by over 50 percent. Maximum tsunami amplitudes reach up to 6.6 m near central and eastern Mediterranean coastlines, while the northern Aegean and the western Mediterranean remain mostly shielded by landmasses in all scenarios. We use the time-dependent seafloor deformation of the worst-case scenario among those considered to highlight differences between static and time-dependent sourcing for tsunami modelling. The results show up to 12 percent higher maximum wave heights in the near-field and slightly lower tsunamis in the far-field compared to a static sourcing, while the waves arrive later. The same scenario is further extended to a large-scale, fully coupled 3-D earthquake-tsunami model that captures dynamic rupture, seismic and acoustic wave propagation, and time-dependent tsunami generation. The fully coupled simulation reveals complex interactions between acoustic and tsunami waves during the early generation phase, including dispersion and wave conversions between seismic and ocean-acoustic waves, which static or linked models do not capture. Overall, our results provide quantitative evidence that source complexity, both spatial and temporal, affects tsunami near–field amplitudes, far–field decay, and arrival metrics, with important implications for physics–based tsunami hazard assessment along the Libyan and Egyptian coasts, the southern Aegean, and eastern Mediterranean shorelines. These results also highlight the value of integrating 3-D dynamic rupture modelling with tsunami simulations and enhance our understanding of tsunami generation mechanisms.",
