        "abstract": "Full-waveform inversion (FWI) is a widely used method for high-resolution velocity modeling. However, when offsets are limited or low-frequency content is insufficient, seismic data are often dominated by reflections, and migration components tend to dominate the inversion gradient. This weakens the tomographic components needed for updating deep long-wavelength structures and increases the risk of convergence to local minima. To address this issue, we propose a scattering-angle-based spatiotemporal filtering strategy for FWI. In this method, scattering angles are estimated from local structural dips and wavefield propagation directions. During gradient computation, angle-domain filtering is applied to selectively retain gradient components within a target scattering-angle range, thereby improving the recovery of the low-wavenumber background velocity model. Unlike conventional wavenumber-domain approaches, the proposed strategy operates directly in the time–space domain and avoids the additional computational and storage cost associated with frequent Fourier transforms and related nonstationary filtering operations. Numerical experiments on the Overthrust and Marmousi models show that the proposed method improves background-velocity recovery and remains stable in noisy data cases. The models optimized by the proposed method provide more suitable initial models for subsequent conventional FWI and help improve its convergence behavior and final inversion results.",
