        "abstract": "Grounded-wire transient electromagnetic (GW-TEM) surveys are strongly affected by the primary field and the receiving system transfer characteristics in the early-time window. These effects obscure effective secondary field information and limit shallow investigation capability. To address this problem, this paper proposes a joint processing method that combines differential-measurement-based primary field removal with Tikhonov-regularized inverse filtering. Near the zero-coupling position, the primary field is sensitive to small spatial offsets, whereas the secondary field varies only slightly. This property allows the primary field response to be extracted and removed through differential measurement. A transfer model is then constructed from the measured primary field response and the measured transmitting-current waveform. This model is used to correct the amplitude and phase distortions introduced by the receiving system. Fixed anomalous-loop experiments show that the proposed method advances the effective observation onset time of the secondary field from about 300 μs to within 70 μs. The corrected early-time response also agrees well with the theoretical decay law. Airborne survey-line results further show that the shallow electrical structure becomes clearer after joint processing. The lateral variation trend at a depth of about 200 m is generally consistent with the reference data. These results demonstrate that the proposed method effectively improves early-time signal recovery in GW-TEM surveys and provides a more reliable data basis for high-resolution shallow exploration.",
