        "abstract": "Seismic tomography based on the eikonal equation and the adjoint-state method has attracted increasing attention because of its low memory requirements, high computational efficiency, and flexibility in handling complex media. To accommodate irregular subsurface geometries, such as foothills and rugged bathymetry, this framework has been extended to curvilinear coordinate systems with boundary-conforming grids. However, in the presence of point sources, both the topography-dependent eikonal equation (TDEE) for traveltime computation and the transport equation for adjoint-state modeling suffer from source singularities, because both explicitly involve the traveltime gradient, which becomes unbounded at the source. Although singularities in the traveltime field can be mitigated by eikonal factorization, singularities in adjoint-state kernels are commonly treated by normalizing two adjoint simulations with different boundary conditions, which doubles the computational cost. To address this limitation, we develop an advection-equation-based adjoint formulation that eliminates source singularities in adjoint-state kernels using only a single adjoint simulation. We derive the corresponding topography-dependent advection equation (TDAE) in curvilinear coordinates and propose an efficient Godunov upwind finite-difference scheme for the resulting elliptically anisotropic advection problem. Synthetic test and applications to 2-D wide-angle ocean-bottom seismometer (OBS) data from the mid-Cayman spreading center (MCSC) show that the proposed method produces smoother and more balanced adjoint-state kernels than the conventional transport-equation-based formulation while maintaining the same computational cost. Tomographic inversion using these improved kernels recovers more accurate velocity models and converges faster.",
