        "abstract": "The conventional finite-element (FE) modeling for marine controlled-source electromagnetic (MCSEM) typically employs hexahedral or tetrahedral meshes. However, the hexahedral meshes are difficult to accurately depict undulating terrain or complex subsurface anomalies. Conversely, the tetrahedral meshes offer greater geometric adaptability but have to obey stricter requirements for mesh quality control. To satisfy boundary conditions, such as a Dirichlet boundary condition, one needs to add large padding areas to extend the boundary, resulting in numerous redundant tetrahedral elements that significantly reduce computational efficiency and increase memory consumption. In this study, we introduce a hybrid discretization strategy that integrates unstructured tetrahedral grids with the octree-based hexahedral ones, using unstructured tetrahedra for fine discretization in the target areas while employing hexahedral meshes in the padding area with an octree mesh to accelerate boundary extension. Our method provides a more reasonable element distribution and overcomes the tradeoff issue between geometric flexibility and computational efficiency in MCSEM modeling. This will substantially reduce the computational cost and memory usage. After the theoretical development, we first validate the accuracy of our hybrid mesh FE method using a uniform half-space model. Then, we compare our results with those from a conventional FE method to demonstrate the advantages of our method in terms of computational efficiency and memory consumption. Finally, we apply our algorithm to an irregular saddle model with undulating seafloor terrain to further demonstrate its ability to simulate complex terrain and subsurface complex anomalies.",
