        "abstract": "Acoustic impedance inversion is an important tool for seismic quantitative interpretation and reservoir characterization. However, its reliability is affected by multiple factors, among which seismic wavelet uncertainty and amplitude loss caused by attenuation during wave propagation are particularly important. In conventional poststack inversion workflows, the seismic wavelet is usually estimated before inversion, and attenuation compensation is often treated as a separate preprocessing step. Such a decoupled treatment may transfer wavelet estimation and amplitude compensation errors into the inverted impedance model, especially when the effective seismic wavelet varies with time in attenuative media. This study develops a nonlinear blind acoustic impedance inversion method based on automatic differentiation for poststack seismic data. The proposed method treats the seismic wavelet as an unknown variable and updates it together with the acoustic impedance model, thereby reducing the dependence on a prescribed wavelet. A smooth quality factor model estimated from velocity information through an empirical relationship is introduced into the nonstationary forward modeling process to describe the main amplitude loss caused by attenuation. The impedance model is linked to seismic data through the nonlinear acoustic reflectivity relation at normal incidence, rather than through a linearized approximation. The coupled nonlinear problem is optimized using an alternating Adam strategy with gradients evaluated by automatic differentiation, while structural and wavelet constraints are incorporated to stabilize the inversion, promote geologically continuous impedance variations, and obtain physically reasonable wavelet estimates. Synthetic experiments show that the proposed method can improve impedance recovery under seismic wavelet uncertainty and amplitude loss caused by attenuation, especially compared with fixed wavelet inversion using an inaccurate wavelet. Applications to two field datasets further illustrate the practical applicability of the method and the geological plausibility of the resulting impedance variations when the seismic wavelet is difficult to estimate reliably.",
