        "abstract": "Elastic reverse-time migration (ERTM) solves the two-way elastic wave equation to image both PP and converted PS waves. In conventional ERTM, filtering can attenuate the low-frequency noise caused by near $180~^{\\circ }$ C cross correlations, but low-wavenumber and velocity-gradient artifacts still degrade the images. De-primary ERTM (Dp-ERTM) performs wavefield decomposition and forms images by cross correlating the downgoing source-side wavefield with the upgoing receiver-side wavefield. This strategy effectively suppresses low-wavenumber and velocity-gradient artifacts. However, in complex structural settings, up- and downgoing wave decomposition cannot reliably distinguish reflection energy from transmission energy. As a result, reflections from steeply dipping structures may leak into discarded components, attenuating their imaging energy. To address these limitations, we propose a dip-guided multidirectional wavefield decomposition ERTM imaging condition. A polarity-preserved outward normal dip-angle field is estimated from the structure tensor and velocity-gradient polarity. Multidirectional wavefield decomposition is implemented with Hilbert transforms along arbitrary directions. The dip-angle information constrains the directions of the wavefield components used in the imaging cross correlation, thereby preserving effective reflection energy consistent with complex structural trends. Numerical examples on the simple salt-dome, BP, and SEAM models show that the method achieves artifact suppression comparable to Dp-ERTM while better preserving the energy that illuminates dipping structures steeply.",
