        "abstract": "Passive microseismic elastic full-waveform inversion (EFWI) is highly nonlinear and prone to cycle-skipping due to phase mismatch, and is further complicated by unknown source parameters that couple with the velocity model. To address these challenges, we propose a dynamically regularized elastic wavefield reconstruction inversion (DR-EWRI) method for estimating source location and the velocity model without prior assumptions. We design a dynamic regularization factor based on the model-gradient magnitude and combine it with a residual-energy-based adaptive weighting mechanism. This strategy automatically adjusts the smoothing strength during inversion, stabilizing convergence in noisy regions while preserving sharp velocity contrasts. We introduce the multisource equations into a block-diagonal parallel structure, enabling independent wavefield updates and simultaneous inversion of multiple events, which enables efficient parallel implementation. A dual-loop strategy alternately updates the source parameters and velocity model, effectively relaxing the wave-equation constraint and broadening the convergence basin. Synthetic tests demonstrate that DR-EWRI outperforms traditional EFWI, EWRI, and EWRI with source estimation (SE-EWRI) approaches in terms of velocity model accuracy, source location error, and computational efficiency. In field applications, the inversion results for 89 hydraulic fracturing events show good consistency with logging velocities and travel-time-based locations. In addition, the overall computational efficiency improves by approximately 50% compared with SE-EWRI, further confirming the stability and reliability of the proposed method.",
