        "abstract": "The seismic inversion is an essential step in reservoir prediction and fluid identification. Traditional prestack amplitude versus offset (AVO) inversion mainly relies on velocity signals. Although this technique is relatively mature, it suffers from low resolution and poor noise resistance under complex geological conditions. Moreover, the seismic inversion based solely on velocity signals is often insufficient to meet the increasingly demanding objectives of hydrocarbon exploration and development. This study proposes a joint inversion method integrating both velocity and acceleration signals. Based on the approximate expression of the Zoeppritz equations and the Levenberg–Marquardt (LM) algorithm, we construct an objective function for the two types of signals. To cope with the nondifferentiability of the LM algorithm at the zero point, we introduce the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMMs) to optimize the inversion process. The effectiveness and performance of the proposed method are then validated through both synthetic data and real data applications. The test results show that the joint inversion has significantly reduced the average relative errors in the inversion of P-wave velocity, S-wave velocity, and density compared to those from single-signal inversion. In addition, it exhibits stronger noise resistance. This joint inversion approach leverages the stability of low-frequency velocity signals and the high resolution of acceleration signals. Consequently, it yields more reliable results and provides a solid foundation for subsequent reservoir prediction.",
