        "authors": "Yun Zhao, Xiaotao Wen, Chunlan Xie, Alexey Stovas, Dongyong Zhou, Chuan Luo",
        "abstract": "Brittleness index (BI) is a critical parameter for unconventional shale reservoir evaluation and hydraulic fracturing design. Existing seismic inversion schemes either estimate BI indirectly or still use first-order reflectivity approximations, suffering from error accumulation, weak-contrast limitations, and underutilization of wide-angle amplitude information. To mitigate these limitations, this study develops a direct BI inversion method based on a quadratic PP-wave reflection approximation. Starting from the quadratic reflection in terms of P-wave velocity, S-wave velocity, and density (QPSD), we first derive a quadratic expression involving Young's modulus, Poisson's ratio, and density (QYPD). Then, BI is introduced explicitly into this framework to obtain a quadratic reflection approximation, denoted as QBYD, achieving direct inversion of BI, Young's modulus, and density. Numerical analyses demonstrate that the QBYD reflection equation maintains higher accuracy than conventional formulations over a wide range of incidence angles. To improve sampling stability under prior constraints, a seismic inversion method is developed using preconditioned Crank-Nicolson Markov chain Monte Carlo sampling with the Lp sparse constraint (pCN-MCMC-LpSC). Synthetic and field examples demonstrate that the proposed method can provide more reliable BI prediction with improved forward accuracy and posterior uncertainty assessment.",
