        "title": "A Homogenized and Declustered Earthquake Catalog for Continental Chile",
        "doi": "10.1785/0220250449",
        "authors": "Gabriel Candia, Jorge Macedo",
        "abstract": "Abstract We present a unified earthquake catalog for continental Chile (15°–60° S), integrating 12 global, regional, and historical datasets within a documented workflow for seismic hazard applications. Earthquake events are consolidated through a depuration scheme that resolves duplicates using explicit ranking and event-priority criteria, and are classified into different earthquake zones and mechanisms using geometrical and tectonic information. A central contribution of this work is the critical evaluation of four declustering algorithms, including Gardner and Knopoff (1974), Reasenberg (1985), and Poulos et al. (2019), and the nearest-neighbor method of Zaliapin and Ben-Zion (2013), which, although used previously, is evaluated here within a unified, multicatalog framework for Chile. For each method, we quantify aftershock removal, identify misclassified aftershocks, and assess temporal consistency through interevent time and event count tests. The declustering results show that recurrence estimates are consistent at aggregated scales, but can diverge in regions dominated by major aftershock sequences, highlighting where catalog processing choices most affect the inferred magnitude recurrence parameters. The workflow used to construct the catalog is fully documented and can be reproduced for other regional earthquake catalogs.",
