        "abstract": "Abstract Amid global warming, coastal regions face escalating risks from extreme meteorological events, yet conventional oceanographic monitoring struggles to deliver sufficient resolution under harsh conditions. This study exploits short-period seismometers for passive nearshore wind–wave monitoring via microseismic noise generated by air–sea–solid Earth interactions. A six-station microarray deployed along Hainan Island, China, from 14 September to 15 October 2024, captured signals across three distinct regimes: Tropical Storm Soulik, Quiescent Period, and a sustained strong-wind period. Power spectral density, time–frequency, and polarization analyses revealed regime-dependent microseismic responses—wind–wave dominance during Soulik, tidal modulation in calm intervals, and combined influences during moderate winds. Strong correlations emerged between spectral energy and both wave height and wind speed. A random forest model fusing spectral and tidal features predicted significant wave height (mean absolute error, MAE = 0.117 m) and wind speed (MAE = 0.861 m/s) on independent test data, outperforming traditional empirical spectral models (MAE = 0.133 m for wave height). Secondary microseisms (0.2–0.8 Hz) proved highly sensitive to wind–wave dynamics, whereas long-period signals were modulated by coastal reflections and tides. This cost-effective approach provides a complementary framework to conventional methods, enhancing real-time marine hazard monitoring and studies of Earth system coupling.",
