        "title": "Multi‐Stage Lithospheric Destruction of the North China Craton: Evidence From a Magnetotelluric Array Study",
        "doi": "10.1029/2024jb029739",
        "authors": "Gaofeng Ye, Baochun Li, Sheng Jin, Wenbo Wei, Letian Zhang, Hao Dong, Chengliang Xie, Yaotian Yin, Shaohuai Sun, Jien Dong, Cheng Liu, Ce Yang, Haoxiang Yin",
        "abstract": "Abstract To investigate lithospheric destruction of the North China Craton (NCC), we obtained a three‐dimensional resistivity model using a magnetotelluric (MT) array covering the Western Block, the Trans‐North China Orogen, and the Eastern Block. Using the Nernst‐Einstein, Arrhenius equations and Hashin‐Shtrikman ( HS ) bounds, we quantitatively estimated temperature and melt fraction from the resistivity model. The model reveals two low‐resistivity anomaly systems separated by the resistive Taihang Uplift. The northern Western Block and its peripheral grabens display a large‐scale anomaly, whereas the southern Western Block remains resistive. The Eastern Block also shows an upper mantle low‐resistivity anomaly. The low‐resistivity anomaly in the Western Block is localized and multi‐causal: it results from partial melting, water, and carbon dioxide. In contrast, the anomaly in the Eastern Block is primarily controlled by Mesozoic westward subduction and retreat of the Paleo‐Pacific Plate, causing lithospheric thinning and partial melting, with the Tanlu Fault Zone and southern Taihang Uplift as melt conduits. The NCC destruction is multi‐stage: Mesozoic Paleo‐Pacific subduction modified the Eastern Block, while Cenozoic far‐field effects of Tibetan Plateau uplift induced anticlockwise rotation of the Western Block, triggering localized upwelling along pre‐existing faults and forming Cenozoic volcanoes and rift basins. MT data provide key electrical evidence for this heterogeneous, multi‐stage destruction process.",
