        "doi": "10.1029/2026jb034033",
        "authors": "Lulu Zhao, Haoran Meng, Xiaofeng Liang, Wentao Li, Xiuyu Liang",
        "abstract": "Abstract Understanding temporal changes in subsurface properties is crucial for assessing the impact of environmental changes on the shallow subsurface. In this study, we investigate temporal variations in seismic velocity and relative Q ‐value changes associated with attenuation variations in the Taklimakan Desert from July 2017 to October 2019, using seismic data recorded by three broadband seismographs. Velocity changes are estimated through Rayleigh wave ellipticity analysis, while attenuation variations are derived from road traffic‐generated seismic signals. Our results reveal clear seasonal fluctuations, with varying by up to 4% and by as much as 10%. These changes strongly correlate with temperature fluctuations with negligible time delay, indicating that thermoelastic strain governs long‐term changes. Additionally, transient wetting events are accompanied by sharp drops in both and , consistent with reduced frame stiffness and increased attenuation due to wave‐induced fluid flow. These findings underscore the dominant role of environmental forcing on subsurface seismic properties variations in the Taklimakan Desert.",
