        "abstract": "SUMMARY The horizontal magnetic interstation transfer function ($\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$) offers distinct advantages in magnetotelluric (MT) studies due to its reduced susceptibility to shallow galvanic distortions. While its utility in constraining subsurface conductivity through 2-D inversions is established, its resolution characteristics in 3-D scenarios—particularly regarding depth sensitivity and lateral recovery—remain poorly quantified. Here, we conduct a systematic investigation of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$ through 3-D forward modelling, sensitivity analysis, and inversion of synthetic models, followed by a validation using long-period MT array data from the Eastern Pamir. Key findings reveal: (1) $\\mathbf {M}$ exhibits response patterns analogous to the impedance tensor ($\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ Z}}$), with an inverse component correspondence where diagonal elements of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ Z}}$ align with off-diagonal elements of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$. (2) Reference station placement critically controls the inversion performance of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$—locations above conductive anomalies distort anomalous currents and degrade data fit, whereas resistive homogeneous backgrounds enhance lateral resolution. (3) $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$ resolves shallow anomalies with lateral boundary definition and resistivity contrast recovery superior to those of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ Z}}$ and the tipper ($\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ T}}$) in sparse arrays. (4) Sensitivity tests indicate that $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$ provides limited resolution for deep structures beyond the effective inductive scale, where its recovery capability diminishes significantly compared to $\\mathbf {Z}$. (5) Application to field data demonstrates that incorporating $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$ improves the resolution of complex shallow structures and enhances the fit of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ Z}}$ data, with the joint inversion of $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ Z}}+\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}+\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ T}}$ yielding the most robust electrical model. We recommend using multiple reference stations for field data inversion to mitigate the impact of variations in reference site locations. This work establishes a quantitative framework for deploying $\\mathbf {\\boldsymbol{ M}}$ in field surveys and 3-D inversions.",
