        "abstract": "Abstract The heterogeneous coupling distribution on subduction interface not only influences the slip distribution of megathrust earthquakes but also plays a critical role in facilitating intraplate strike-slip events. On 16 July 2025, an Mw 7.3 strike-slip earthquake struck the Shumagin segment of the Alaska Peninsula, raising questions about its seismogenic fault orientation and mechanical origin. Using artificial intelligence-enhanced detection and relocation of the seismic sequence, we constructed an east–west trending, south-dipping, curved seismogenic fault model. Joint inversion of teleseismic waveforms and Global Navigation Satellite System data reveals a predominantly sinistral strike-slip mechanism, and the rupture was confined largely beneath the subduction interface, indicating that it is an intraplate event. Together with the nearby 2020 Mw 7.6 dextral strike-slip earthquake, the two events form a conjugate fault system. We demonstrate that both earthquakes, though differing in strike direction, can be explained by the stress perturbations arising from along-strike and along-dip variations in megathrust coupling, acting in concert with the background stresses from slab pull and plate bending. These findings underscore the widespread influence of coupling heterogeneity in influencing strike-slip earthquake generation in subduction zones and call for a reassessment of related seismic hazards in regions such as the Semidi segment.",
