        "abstract": "Precise characterization of small-scale geological heterogeneities is fundamental to seismic exploration; however, its success relies heavily on high-fidelity diffraction imaging. Conventionally, weak diffraction signals are often obscured by dominant specular reflections, making robust wavefield separation a critical prerequisite for target identification. Current mainstream paradigms, primarily based on post-migration dip-domain separation, are inherently limited by their high sensitivity to velocity model inaccuracies and the significant computational overhead associated with multidimensional gather extraction. Consequently, there is a pressing need to develop high-precision pre-stack separation techniques. Nevertheless, this remains challenging due to complex wavefield interference in raw data, where conventional methods struggle to distinguish deep, weak diffractions from kinematically similar reflections, often resulting in severe residual noise or signal degradation. To address these limitations, we propose a novel framework operating in a high-dimensional 3D slowness-frequency-wavenumber (p, f, k) feature space. By leveraging the distinct topological differences between reflections and diffractions in this transformed domain, the proposed method effectively decouples interfering energy components without relying on precise velocity models. Benchmarking against synthetic and field datasets demonstrates that our approach significantly suppresses reflection leakage while preserving the structural integrity of diffracted wavefields. Results indicate that the proposed p − f − k topological separation provides a more robust and computationally efficient solution for imaging complex fractures and small-scale reservoirs.",
