        "abstract": "Accurately solving the multiple components of acoustic wavefields in phased-array logging is significant for wavefield separation and detailed formation characterization. Traditional methods typically rely on elastic wave equations for meshed finite element method (FEM) solutions, which are computationally expensive. Most existing deep learning methods generally lack physical constraints and rely heavily on extensive wavefield data. Furthermore, the inherent coupling properties of the elastic wave equation lead to model failure in solving coupled P- and S-waves without explicit constraints. To address these issues, this work presents a residual-adaptive (RA) physics-informed neural network (RAPINN) for componentwise solution of overlapping multisource forward acoustic wavefields in the phased-array logging scenarios with limited snapshot data. First, a hybrid-driven framework integrating prior physical knowledge with sparse data constraints is proposed. It embeds the governing equations describing wavefield propagation behavior characteristics into the loss function. By training with sparse data, wavefield prediction accuracy is enhanced while the required wavefield data is significantly reduced. Second, the coupled elastic wave equations are decomposed via Helmholtz decomposition to derive potential-based wave equations, enhancing the explicit constraints for P- and S-waves. Third, a pointwise weight assignment strategy based on a residual adaptive mechanism is proposed. The pointwise weights of the sampling points are adapted according to their residual gradients, thereby enhancing the focus on details in difficult-to-learn regions. Finally, exploratory attempts are made to investigate the fluid–solid structure of the borehole, establishing a multisource coupled elastic wavefield surrogate model based on RAPINN under limited data. Numerical examples conducted under different source quantities, synthetic wavefield deflection angles, boundary conditions (BCs), and media demonstrate that the RAPINN provides a feasible reference for acoustic wavefield solving problems.",
