        "title": "Adaptive Window-Enhanced ESISTEM-VCE for 3-D Coseismic Deformation of the 2010 Mw 8.8 Maule Earthquake",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3704441",
        "authors": "Yang Liu, Yang Jiang, Luyun Xiong, Yangmao Wen, Caijun Xu",
        "abstract": "The ESISTEM with variance component estimation (ESISTEM-VCE) method based on the local uniform-strain assumption faces limitations in the inversion accuracy for near-field high-gradient regions when fusing global navigation satellite system (GNSS) and interferometric synthetic aperture radar (InSAR) data to retrieve coseismic 3-D deformation fields, as its fixed-window design cannot adapt to the inhomogeneous deformation fields induced by strong earthquakes. To address this limitation, an adaptive window-enhanced ESISTEM-VCE method constrained by the deformation gradient is proposed. An iterative strategy is adopted to optimize deformation gradient estimation. First, initial deformation gradients are derived from InSAR data. Subsequently, based on the preliminary inversion results, the composite 3-D deformation gradient is iteratively updated, and the optimal reference window for each target point is adaptively determined using the reference point selection function. Through synthetic experiments, different reference point selection functions are systematically evaluated. The results indicate that the sigmoid function provides the best overall balance of inversion accuracy among the three deformation components. Compared with the traditional method, the root-mean-square errors (RMSEs) in the east–west, south–north, and vertical directions are reduced by approximately 18.3%, 3.6%, and 15.3%, respectively. In addition, the synthetic experiments further identify the optimal ranges of reference point numbers, with the number of GNSS reference points ranging from 10 to 60 and the number of InSAR reference points ranging from 20 to 80. A reasonable smoothing radius should be chosen during the spatial smoothing of reference point numbers. The proposed method is then applied to the 2010 Maule Mw 8.8 earthquake in Chile, successfully retrieving a high-precision coseismic 3-D deformation field. The results show that, compared with the traditional fixed-window ESISTEM-VCE method, the proposed method can more effectively recover large-magnitude coseismic deformation signals in the near field of strong earthquakes. Quantitative comparisons with GNSS observations indicate that the RMSEs in the east–west, south–north, and vertical directions are reduced by approximately 4.8%, 19.1%, and 0.9%, respectively. Moreover, comparisons with field-measured vertical deformation show an RMSE reduction of approximately 9.8%.",
