        "abstract": "High-fidelity seismic imaging of steeply dipping structures remains a formidable challenge. Accurately characterizing these formations is crucial for unlocking hidden hydrocarbon reservoirs. However, conventional migration methods frequently fall short, suffering from severely limited illumination, pervasive crosstalk noise, and low imaging accuracy when delineating near-vertical reflectors. To overcome these persistent obstacles, this paper proposes a novel, targeted joint primary-prismatic wave imaging methodology operating within a real and imaginary dual-domain framework. Our approach innovatively decouples primary and prismatic wavefields. By calculating primary waves strictly in the real domain and confining prismatic waves to the imaginary domain, this strategy effectively suppresses the severe crosstalk artifacts that plague conventional prismatic wave imaging. Concurrently, it fully preserves the invaluable kinematic information regarding steep structures carried by prismatic waves. Building upon complex-domain wavefield extrapolation, adjoint-state theory, and the Born approximation, we rigorously derive the propagation formulas and specialized imaging conditions necessary for this dual-domain joint imaging. Furthermore, a sophisticated self-adaptive weighting strategy is introduced to dynamically balance the contributions of primary and prismatic imaging results based on local signal energy, thereby guaranteeing a stable, well-balanced representation of both gently dipping beds and near-vertical structures. Compre-hensive numerical tests on the Inverse-T and steeply dipping faults models demonstrate the method's superiority. Compared to conventional reverse time migration (RTM) and standard prismatic wave RTM, our approach successfully eliminates crosstalk noise while significantly enhancing steep reflector illumination and spatial resolution. Finally, applications to two distinct field datasets featuring complex fault systems validate its practical efficacy, proving that this method clearly delineates major and minor steep faults, strengthens structural continuity, and drastically reduces residual imaging artifacts.",
