        "abstract": "Full waveform inversion (FWI) is a powerful seismic imaging technique capable of reconstructing high-resolution subsurface velocity structures. However, the application of FWI in three-dimensional faces several major challenges. This is primarily due to the high memory overhead, computational cost, and susceptibility to cycle skipping in traditional 3DFWI. To address these issues, we propose M-SSIM-3DIFWI, a 3D implicit FWI method that integrates the multi-scale structural similarity index measure and implicit neural representation. M-SSIM is employed to quantify the multi-scale structural similarity between predicted and observed seismic data, effectively mitigating cycle skipping while enhancing inversion resolution and accuracy. Implicit neural representation is used to reparameterize the velocity model, allowing for a continuous representation based on learned basis functions that impose implicit regularization. To address memory concerns involved in storing the wavefield, we employ the boundary storage technique. Numerical tests on synthetic and field datasets show that the proposed approach produces inversion results with both high resolution and fidelity, even when low-frequency data are lacking or the initial models are far from accurate. These findings demonstrate that M-SSIM-3DIFWI with boundary storage is an effective, robust, and memory-efficient method suitable for large-scale 3D seismic imaging.",
