        "title": "Two-Stage Focusing Inversion of Gravity Data Using Student’s t Distribution and Iterative Hard Thresholding",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3705426",
        "authors": "Guomin Peng, Xuepeng Wang, Tao Hu",
        "abstract": "The conventional gravity inverse problem is solved through simultaneous minimization of both data misfit and model regularization terms. The data misfit is typically represented by L2 -norm measure of the data residuals between the modeled and observed gravity data with an assumption of a Gaussian distribution of residuals. However, the L2 -norm of data residuals is sensitive to the noise, particularly outliers, present in the data, and the probability distribution of data residuals may not follow a Gaussian distribution in reality. The traditional sparsity-promoting inversion approaches usually use non- L2 -norm measures for the construction of model regularization term, in which regularization parameter used to balance the data misfit and model regularization items needs to be correctly estimated. Moreover, in the non- L2 -norm regularization term, there is usually a parameter, which is closely related to the sparsity of the recovered model, to be estimated accurately in advance. To alleviate these problems, this article proposes a two-stage focusing gravity inversion. In the first stage, assuming a Student’s t distribution of data residuals, a density model is obtained by minimizing the data misfit only. In the second stage, the model generated in the first stage is used as an initial model to obtain a density model with a clearer boundary by means of iterative hard thresholding (IHT). The synthetic example demonstrates that, compared with traditional Lp -norm inversion, this proposed method produces a density model reconstruction with reduced model misfit (RMS m ) and improved Pearson correlation coefficient ( R ) for the gravity data contaminated with non-Gaussian noise. The proposed approach is also tested on the measured gravity data from the San Nicolas deposit, showing that the reconstructed sulfide body has a good consistency with known information.",
