        "title": "Investigating ULF Geomagnetic Anomalies Before the 2013 Lushan Earthquake Using Weighted Multichannel Singular Spectrum Analysis",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3705524",
        "authors": "Wenqi Chen, Kaiguang Zhu, Dedalo Marchetti, Ting Wang, Yiqun Zhang, Donghua Zhang, Baiyi Yang, Pu Wang, Yuqi Cheng",
        "abstract": "In this study, ground-based geomagnetic station data were analyzed to investigate the preparation phase of the 2013 Lushan earthquake (Mw 6.7). Previous studies assigned equal weight to each station during data fusion decomposition possibly reducing the accuracy of anomaly extraction due to unknown noise differences among stations. To address this issue, we propose a Weighted Multichannel Singular Spectrum Analysis (WMSSA) method, in which station weights are determined by their signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs) to enhance the extraction of common-mode signals. WMSSA was applied to geomagnetic observations from the Chengdu and the Tianshui stations during the 90 days before the earthquake. The relation of the geomagnetic anomalies with the Lushan earthquake is explored, and the results are compared with those obtained through conventional Multichannel Singular Spectrum Analysis (MSSA). The cumulative geomagnetic anomalies trend exhibited two acceleration phases before the mainshock: from -42 to -31 days, and from -6 to -2 days. Under this two-station, casebased configuration, differences in geomagnetic anomaly energy were observed between the two stations located at different epicentral distances. The possibility that the detected anomalies were primarily caused by geomagnetic activity was found to be unlikely based on geomagnetic indices. Subsequently, we analyzed the earthquake events and found that the seismic Benioff strain trend representative of the lithosphere also exhibited two growths before the mainshock, consistent with the cumulative geomagnetic anomalies trend. Furthermore, the temporal correlation between geomagnetic anomalies and other geo-layer parameters suggested that the geomagnetic anomalies are most likely associated with lithospheric processes preceding the Lushan earthquake, which can be interpreted within the framework of a lithosphere-atmosphere-ionosphere coupling (LAIC) model.",
