        "title": "Three-Layer Physics-Constrained Riemannian Manifold Deep Network for Microseismic Event Localization in Coal Mines",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3705759",
        "authors": "Kai Zhan, Hao Luo, Yishan Pan, Rui Xu, Jiajun Chen",
        "abstract": "The precise localization of microseismic sources is crucial for enhancing safety in coal mine surveillance. Traditional physics-based algorithms deliver metre-level precision but require seconds-to-minutes per event, whereas AI-based approaches achieve millisecond inference with compromised accuracy, creating an accuracy-efficiency dilemma. To bridge this dilemma, we propose RiemannianGF - a graph-convolutional network that embeds the velocity model as a Riemannian manifold and is steered by a three-layer physics constraint: (i) structural constraint, geodesic distances derived from manifold travel times define the graph topology; (ii) training constraint, synthetic travel-time pairs generated by forward modelling provide physically consistent supervision; and (iii) loss constraint, a physics-regularised term penalises residual travel-time misfits during optimisation. This integrated treatment of geometry, data and loss enables metre-level accuracy with millisecond-scale inference, thereby simultaneously resolving the deficiencies of traditional and AI methods. Compared with state-of-the-art AI baselines, RiemannianGF achieves higher localisation accuracy without sacrificing computational efficiency. Experimental results on both the 3-D Marmousi benchmark and field data from Dongtan Coal Mine confirm its superiority in localisation error and inference speed. By explicitly embedding physics at multiple scales, RiemannianGF closes the accuracy gap while retaining the speed advantage of AI methods, thereby advancing practical coal-mine hazard mitigation.",
