        "abstract": "The electromagnetic (EM) methods are highly sensitive to low-resistivity anomalies, but the attenuation nature of EM diffusion and the inherent nonuniqueness of inverse problems often lead to blurred anomaly boundaries and loss of resolution at depth. Although regularization constraints are widely used to improve geological plausibility, the conventional approaches rely heavily on prescribed prior information and struggle to adapt to complex structures, frequently resulting in oversmoothing or inversion artifacts. In this study, we propose an adaptive regularized inversion framework that integrates a deep-learning-based regularization network with an imaging enhancement network to mitigate resolution loss caused by EM diffusion. The regularization network provides a data-driven reference model that is embedded into the objective function, while its uncertainty estimation is used to dynamically weight the regularization during inversion. During the iterative process, the network-generated model is automatically adopted if it yields a lower data misfit, enabling adaptive model updating guided by both physics and learned structural features. After inversion, an imaging enhancement network is applied to further sharpen geological boundaries. The synthetic tests using transient EM (TEM) and magnetotelluric (MT) data demonstrate that the proposed method effectively distinguishes smooth background regions from blocky structures while maintaining high data-fitting accuracy. Application to field TEM data illustrates improved resolution and clearer delineation of low-resistivity structures, highlighting the potential of the framework for enhanced subsurface electrical imaging in complex geological settings.",
