        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3706379",
        "authors": "Chongyang Han, Hui Zhou, Weixin Yang, Yong Zeng, Yu Wang, Shanbo Sheng, Qin Su",
        "abstract": "The acquisition geometry of vertical seismic profiling (VSP) inherently leads to heterogeneous spatial sampling of subsurface reflectors. In particular, fold coverage decreases rapidly with increasing distance from the receiver well, producing severe artifacts in migrated images. Surface seismic data, which are commonly acquired simultaneously in VSP survey areas, are structurally consistent with the VSP imaging targets. Because the dips associated with migration artifacts differ substantially from those of true geological structures imaged by surface-seismic data, these surface-seismic structural attributes can provide robust prior constraints. This article proposes two new migration artifact attenuation methods that exploit such prior information. The first method, termed the dip-angle-difference constraint, compares dip differences between VSP and surface-seismic images; VSP amplitudes are preferentially preserved within a prescribed dip-confidence interval by means of a Gaussian decay weight. The second method is dip-guided Laplacian filtering, which constructs an anisotropic filter from surface-seismic structure tensors and iteratively smooths the VSP image along geologically consistent directions. Reverse time migration (RTM) experiments on synthetic and field data show that both methods effectively suppress migration artifacts, whereas dip-guided Laplacian filtering provides higher computational efficiency and better preservation of structural continuity. The proposed approach therefore offers a robust postprocessing tool for high-precision VSP imaging.",
