        "abstract": "Catastrophic landslides in remote areas often lack on-site monitoring, delaying emergency response. Regional seismic networks enable broad monitoring, but extracting landslide-generated seismic signals (LGSS) from long, noisy continuous recordings remain challenging.We develop SDLNet, a lightweight end-to-end deep learning framework for joint LGSS separation and detection from three-component seismic data. SDLNet adopts a dual encoder-decoder design with bottleneck temporal-context modeling and multi-scale channel attention, together with skip connections to preserve waveform details. The model is trained and evaluated on a self-constructed LGSS dataset with multi-SNR augmentation. The developed model accomplishes improved signal recovery (ISDR) values exceeding 0.75 in 89.25% of the test set, and even under a signal-to-noise ratio of -10 dB, the ISDR value still reaches 0.466. Our results demonstrate that the time-frequency spectrum of SDLNet-separated LGSS provides more reliable insights for analyzing the evolutionary processes of landslides and improves the accuracy of landslide location. Our findings suggest that SDLNet can reliably capture over looked minor pre-event or secondary landslide activity. The heatmap analysis reveals that LGSS and tectonic earthquake signals exhibit similar yet distinguishable feature representations. These results indicate that SDLNet provides an efficient tool for near-real-time landslide rapid reporting from regional seismic networks.",
