        "abstract": "Sedimentary cycle division is crucial for the prediction of high-quality reservoirs. Deep carbonate reservoirs are currently a significant area for oil and gas exploration. Due to severe attenuation of seismic signals in deep layers, existing time-frequency (TF) post-processing methods struggle to accurately characterize the trend of dominant frequency variations caused by changes in formation thickness, which limits the accuracy of cycle division. To improve the energy concentration in time-frequency representation (TFR), this paper proposes the matching chirplet squeezing transform (MCST) for a more accurate division of sedimentary cycle units. First, the instantaneous frequency (IF) and group delay in the short-time Fourier transform domain are calculated to obtain the chirp rate function. Then, within the chirplet transform (CT) domain, the IF band center is derived, and a time-frequency energy focusing domain (TFEFD) is constructed. Subsequently, by designing a matched chirp rate estimator, the local variation rate of the IF trajectory is accurately obtained in the CT domain. Finally, within the TFEFD domain, TF coefficients are reassigned to the true IF trajectory through squeezing transform, yielding the TF spectrum of the MCST. Synthetic signal experiments verify that the proposed MCST achieves high-resolution TFR and exhibits superior noise resistance. Analysis results of MCST applied to a typical sedimentary cycle model and field seismic data from deep carbonates demonstrate that the method effectively enhances the resolution of tuning frequencies and accurately divides sedimentary cycle units. The results align well with log interpretations, indicating that MCST is a promising tool for sedimentary cycle division.",
