        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3708286",
        "authors": "Zijuan Ma, Jun Lu, Shanhui Ma",
        "abstract": "Shear-wave leakage (i.e., Vz noise) severely contaminates the vertical component of ocean bottom node seismic data and masks useful reflections (i.e., reflected P-wave), particularly near offsets where the signal-to-noise overlap is the strongest. Conventional denoising methods often have difficulty separating Vz noise from the reflection signals under such conditions. To address this problem, we developed a Vz noise attenuation method that combines Vz noise event flattening with scale- and direction-constrained thresholding in the contourlet domain. Based on a simplified PS-wave velocity model, we applied the Vz normal moveout correction to the Z component to flatten the Vz noise, which improved its separability from reflection events. Scale and directional adjustment factors, along with a sliding-window estimate of the local signal standard deviation, were then incorporated into an adaptive BayesShrink threshold. A continuous thresholding function was used to extract and reconstruct the Vz noise component from the selected sub-bands, which were then subtracted from the Z component. Applying this procedure to both synthetic and field data indicates that the proposed method effectively suppresses strongly overlapping Vz noise in near-offset regions while preserving useful signals. Quantitative evaluations and denoising results obtained from both datasets further confirm this capability.",
