        "doi": "10.1016/j.epsl.2026.120003",
        "authors": "Gang Song, Lu Yao, Tao Yang, Shengli Ma",
        "abstract": "Frictional properties of granite have been extensively studied to explore the mechanisms of earthquake nucleation in the continental crust. Although theoretical and experimental studies suggest that increasing effective normal stress ( σ n eff ) increases critical stiffness and promotes unstable slip in velocity-weakening faults, few experiments have investigated how σ n eff may affect friction rate parameter ( a – b ) under hydrothermal conditions where deformation mechanisms are complex and fluid-rock interactions are significant. In this study, hydrothermal rotary-shear friction experiments were conducted on granite gouges under σ n eff ranging from 30 to 240 MPa, temperatures of 50–400 °C, pore pressure of 30–40 MPa, and slip velocities of 0.4–1250 μm/s. Results show that the sign of ( a – b ) for granite gouges at slip velocities ≤ 10 μm/s can vary substantially with σ n eff , particularly in temperature regimes representative of shallow depths (∼50 °C) and the base of the seismogenic zone (∼400 °C). These findings imply that the slip stability of granitic faults—whether stable, unstable or conditionally stable—could be largely modulated by σ n eff within these temperature regimes through its influence on the sign of ( a – b ), offering a plausible explanation for the complex fault slip behavior observed in nature (e.g., regular earthquakes, slow slip, and tremors). Data extrapolation also suggests that σ n eff can significantly affect the earthquake nucleation depth on granitic faults. These results provide valuable insights into the mechanisms of various fault slip behaviors, suggesting that the σ n eff dependence of ( a – b ) are worth incorporating into future earthquake modeling.",
