        "title": "Point defects as a control on the viscosity jump in the lower mantle",
        "doi": "10.1016/j.epsl.2026.120008",
        "authors": "Peixue Dou, John Brodholt, Lidunka Vočadlo, Yunguo Li, Huaiwei Ni",
        "abstract": "Our understanding of the viscosity of the lower mantle is still quite uncertain, as the dominant ductile deformation mechanism (diffusion creep and pure climb creep) remains heavily debated, leading to different models predicting quite different viscosity profiles with depth. Some of these models are difficult to reconcile with mineralogical constraints. However, a key missing parameter for constraining lower-mantle viscosity is the concentration of point defects. In particular, the concentration of Si vacancies, which are generally believed to be rate limiting in diffusion-controlled creep appropriate for the lower mantle. In this study, we calculated the concentration of Si vacancies in bridgmanite under lower-mantle conditions using ab initio molecular dynamics calculations and thermodynamic integration techniques. We find that Si vacancy concentrations decrease by one to two orders of magnitude from the top to the mid lower mantle, followed by an increase at greater depth. This leads to a corresponding increase and decrease in viscosity of more than an order of magnitude, consistent with many lower mantle viscosity profiles, and allows us to constrain the grain size of the lower mantle to ∼ 1 mm (for diffusion creep) and/or the average deviatoric stress to ∼1 MPa (for pure climb creep). However, variations in Si vacancy concentrations alone cannot explain sudden or larger changes in viscosity, such as a low viscosity channel below 660 km followed by a sharp increase at 1000 km as suggested in some models, or other models predicting increases in viscosity at about 1000 km. In these cases a compositional origin is required. However, the general increase and decrease of viscosity with depth seen in many models is readily explainable by the thermodynamically controlled vacancy concentration and a small increase in grain size.",
