        "abstract": "Earth‘s Inner Core Boundary (ICB) may affect the generation of the present geomagnetic field through the growth of the inner core. The precipitation of the liquid outer core should cause the growth of the inner core (Nimmo, 2015) and it is thought that it generates a mushy layer at the ICB. The structure of the ICB has been studied mainly by analyzing seismic waves traversing the ICB but the scarcity of seismic waves that cover the entire ICB surface is difficult. Here we use PKIIKP waves observed at antipodes. We use the spectral element method and adjoint method to calculate the sensitivity kernels of the antipodal PKIIKP phase and model the heterogeneous P-wave velocity structure at the ICB. We found that there is a region where the Vp is lower than that of the one-dimensional model in South America. Because the Vs heterogeneity at the ICB is small, these low Vp regions at the ICB may be considered mushy regions. The South American region coincides with the geomagnetic anomaly observed at the surface of the Earth, which suggests that there is a close connection between the possible mushy region and the generation of the geomagnetic field.",
