        "title": "PI-SGF: Physics-Informed Swin-GeoFormer for Unsupervised Pore Aspect Ratio Prediction in Reservoir Characterization",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3691776",
        "authors": "Wenbiao Yang, Wenli Shang, Lianhai Wang, Zhiquan Liu",
        "abstract": "Pore aspect ratio (α) is a key microstructural parameter that controls elastic wave propagation in reservoir rocks, yet it cannot be directly measured from well logs, and existing estimation methods rely on computationally expensive iterative inversion or pseudo-labels. This paper presents PI-SGF (Physics-Informed Swin-GeoFormer), a physics-informed framework that couples log-sequence learning with the Berryman–Gassmann rock-physics model as a differentiable constraint, enabling fully unsupervised learning of α alongside supervised estimation of porosity (ϕ), clay content (Vclay), and water saturation (WS). The framework further incorporates geology-sensitive sequence modeling and progressive constraint strengthening to preserve formation-boundary information and stabilize the coupled supervised-unsupervised optimization. On strict cross-basin blind tests spanning three geologically distinct basins on two continents (Junggar, Sichuan, and North Sea), PI-SGF achievesR2≥ 0.90 for α against independent inversion-derived references and velocity reconstructionR2> 0.95. These results indicate that α can be estimated from conventional logs in a physically consistent manner under the evaluated cross-basin setting.",
