        "title": "Sparse Joint Inversion of Gravity and Magnetic Data Using Group <i>L</i> <sub>0</sub> -Norm Model Constraint",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3692989",
        "authors": "Dan Zhu, Danping Cao",
        "abstract": "Owing to the nonconvexity of theL0-norm and the associated optimization difficulty, existing sparse joint inversion methods typically construct objective functions using approximating functions of theL0-norm. Although such approaches can produce focused multiphysics models, they often lose the capability of directly measuring and explicitly controlling the volume of the reconstructed models. In fact, theL0-norm provides a direct measure of the model volume. If theL0-norm is used as the sparsity constraint, focused imaging can be achieved while enabling controllable volume compression. However, in joint inversion, separately enforcing independentL0-norm constraints on the density and magnetization models leads to inconsistent sparsification, thereby disrupting the structural coupling targeted by joint inversion. To address this issue, we introduce the groupL0-norm into joint inversion, treating the multiphysics parameters within each cell as a unified parameter group forL0-norm based sparsity measurement and regularization. Building on this idea, we propose a new joint inversion objective function for gravity and magnetic data that simultaneously incorporates the groupL0-norm and the cross-gradient constraint. To stably solve the proposed nonconvex optimization problem, we develop an improved iterative hard-thresholding algorithm, which achieves an asymptotically linear decrease of the groupL0-norm of the reconstructed model while maintaining stable convergence of the convex terms. Tests on synthetic models and field data demonstrate that the proposed method generates density and magnetization models with consistent boundaries and coherent parameter distributions. Moreover, it enables real-time sensing and control of the model volume during inversion, allowing reconstructions of multiphysics models for target bodies with expected volumes.",
