        "title": "An Efficient and Scalable Multigrid Solver for 3-D Natural Source Electromagnetic Diffusion Problems Based on Finite Element Method",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3670297",
        "authors": "Xinhao Chen, Chunming Liu, Rongwen Guo, Yongfei Wang, Jianxin Liu, Wenxiang Liu, Dengkang Wang",
        "abstract": "Multigrid (MG) solvers offer a promising framework for efficient large-scale 3D electromagnetic (EM) diffusion modeling problems, owing to their potential for linear computational complexity with respect to grid size. However, challenges such as the abundant null space of the curl-curl operator and the influence of the air-layer often lead to slow convergence or even divergence in standard MG solvers. To overcome these limitations, we propose a MG solver incorporating a dual-layer block-wise Gauss-Seidel (GS) smoother tailored for finite element (FE) EM diffusion modeling. For the dual-layer block-wise GS smoother, the basic idea can be considered as that the entire computational grid is partitioned intoN-1 local subdomains withNrepresenting the number of cells in thez-direction, with each subdomain consisting of cells of two adjacent horizontal layers. Field components in each local region are updated using a direct solver, guaranteeing the zero current divergence condition. The global solution is then obtained through recursive cycling in thez-direction. We examined the accuracy and efficiency of the proposed solver by comparing it with two state-of-the-art MG-based solvers as FGMRES with auxiliary space Maxwell solver (FGMRES-AMS) and MG with a semi-coarsening strategy (Semi-MG), and a GS preconditoned biconjugate gradient stabilized (BiCGstab) solvers with two divergence correction strategies, based on the Dublin test model 1 (DTM 1) and two real data inversion models (a Cascadia model and a Gonggashan (GGS) model). The results show that our MG solver achieves significantly better numerical performance in both iteration number and computational time. Our method is stable over wide range of periods with less sensitivity to grid stretching and good scalability to grid size.",
