        "abstract": "High-resolution shear-wave velocity (Vs) characterization is essential for mitigating seismic hazards and enhancing infrastructure resilience in coastal megacities; however, conventional active-source methods often prove impractical in soft-sediment, infrastructure-dense environments. This paper presents a borehole-constrained extended spatial autocorrelation–genetic algorithm (ESPAC-GA) workflow that integrates sparse microtremor arrays with minimal drilling investment to achieve robust two-dimensional Vs imaging to 50 m depth. On the western Pacific coastal plain, a 10-node nested triangular array (58-m aperture) was deployed along a 560-m transect, constrained by three 60-m boreholes serving as constraints or for validation. Hyperparameters (population 20, generations 40) were optimized through 50 Monte-Carlo runs, and ensemble averaging of five independent inversions effectively suppressed non-uniqueness. Numerical validation demonstrates layer thickness and Vs errors of approximately 3%; whereas field application—characterized by highly attenuative, water-saturated clays—reveals a characteristic “shallow-sensitive, deep-robust” response: the resulting 2-D Vs profile clearly delineates the marine–lacustrine–tuff sequence and laterally variable bedrock interfaces, with cumulative bedrock depth errors remaining below 6% and bedrock velocity errors of merely - 6.9%. This accuracy meets offshore wind foundation and immersed-tunnel engineering specifications while reducing drilling costs by over 30%. Furthermore, the optimized hyperparameters may serve as a useful initial model for analogous deltaic settings worldwide. This optimized workflow offers a cost-effective, scalable tool for real-time, high-resolution Vs mapping in coastal zones, supporting climate-adaptive urban planning and infrastructure risk assessment.",
