        "authors": "Max W. Schmidt, Nadia Paneva, Andrea Giuliani",
        "abstract": "Deep mantle melting marks the onset of Earth differentiation, yet a unifying framework for how buoyancy-driven mantle upwellings initiate melting and how such incipient melts evolve within the asthenosphere has remained elusive. Here we show that the first melts generated in solid-state mantle upwellings are kimberlitic CO2-rich silicate melts that form at about 250 km depth through oxidation of elemental carbon to CO2. Experiments force kimberlite, ocean-island basalt and mid-ocean-ridge basalt compositions into equilibrium with fertile mantle at adiabatic and super-adiabatic conditions at 7 GPa. The results define a framework in which redox melting at depth yields kimberlitic melts that evolve during ascent through reactive porous flow to higher degrees of melting, lower volatile and incompatible-element contents, and higher SiO2. The framework is consistent with the widespread seismic low-velocity zone near 250 km beneath mid-ocean ridges.",
