        "abstract": "Earth’s early mantle probably existed as a deep, vigorously convecting magma ocean, and its solidification is considered central to the long-term chemical and dynamical evolution of the planet. Yet a notable uncertainty is the grain size of bridgmanite—the dominant lower-mantle phase—whose nucleation behaviour at extreme pressure has remained experimentally inaccessible. Here we show, using large-scale molecular dynamics simulations driven by machine-learning potentials, seeding and enhanced sampling, that crystal–melt interfacial energies of MgSiO3 bridgmanite increase substantially with pressure. In a deep basal magma ocean, this amplified interfacial energy and sluggish cooling may permit centimetre-to-metre-scale bridgmanite crystals. Such crystals could drive efficient fractional crystallization, chemical differentiation and mantle compaction, providing a physical pathway linking lower-mantle material properties to early Earth stratification.",
