        "title": "A Robust Coal–Rock Interface Identification Model for GPR Data With Complex Interference and Low Signal-to-Noise Ratio",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2026.3667878",
        "authors": "Lingzhou Chen, Xianlei Xu, Suping Peng",
        "abstract": "Accurate identification of the coal-rock interface in mines is one of the critical challenges for intelligent and safe coal mining. Air-coupled Ground Penetrating Radar (GPR) has shown unique advantages in coal-rock interface identification. However, constrained by the complex and strong interference geological environment in mines (such as roof supports, gangue in coal seams, faults, and layer pinch-outs), GPR data often suffers from low signal-to-noise ratios (SNR), which severely affects the performance of horizon tracking and directly leads to incorrect horizon identification. This paper proposes a robust coal-rock interface identification model for GPR data with complex interference and low SNR. Compared to conventional coal-rock horizon identification algorithms, this model can: 1) construct an adaptive stacked waveform as a dynamic reference, guiding the tracking by calculating the similarity between the current waveform and this reference, thereby adapting to lateral signal variations and improving the reliability of judgments; 2) implement a jump strategy based on local trend prediction. When the similarity falls below a threshold, indicating tracking difficulties, the algorithm intelligently predicts the current position using the local geological trend estimated from previously tracked horizon points (least squares fitting). This allows it to effectively cross low SNR, structural interference, or data-missing regions while maintaining the continuity of geological structures. Extensive testing and comparative analysis on simulated data with different noise levels, gangue interference, and bad traces, as well as on actual coal-rock interface model data, mine data, and road horizon data, demonstrate that: the proposed method achieves continuous and accurate horizon tracking, especially under low SNR (SNR15%) conditions, where its robustness is significantly superior to traditional tracking methods. The proposed method shows clear advantages in avoiding non-geological 'steps' and preserving geological trends, with fewer jumps and comparable computational efficiency to traditional horizon tracking methods (completing 4200 traces of profile tracking in only 0.215s). Furthermore, it can accurately track low SNR actual mine coal-rock interface horizons and road horizons with pipeline interference, further demonstrating its adaptability in geological radar horizon tracking applications. This research provides an efficient tool for automatic and precise interpretation of GPR coal-rock interfaces under complex conditions, holding significant value for improving geological exploration accuracy and supporting intelligent mining.",
