        "authors": "Ziyu Qin, Shulin Pan",
        "abstract": "Accurate prediction of fracture parameters is a core component in the exploration and development of shale oil and gas. Existing methods suffer from insufficient precision in the quantitative inversion of fracture parameters, and fracture dip angle in particular poses prominent inversion challenges due to its strong nonlinear characteristics and weak seismic response. To address this, this paper proposes a progressive deep learning architecture FracQuantNet to achieve step-by-step quantitative inversion of fracture density and dip angle. This architecture adopts a cascaded ResUNet-ResNet structure. ResUNet extracts multi-scale features through encoder-decoder modules with residual connections, which enables stable prediction of normal fracture weakness and density. ResNet integrates preliminary prediction results with original inputs via residual connections to realize refined estimation of fracture dip angle. To enhance the model’s generalization ability under complex geological conditions, this paper uses the derived relationship between fracture parameters and seismic azimuthal amplitude differences for unsupervised fine-tuning to optimize the network. The inversion accuracy of FracQuantNet is significantly superior to that of traditional methods and single deep learning models, and both the application verification of theoretical models and the application results of actual data confirm that this method possesses high inversion accuracy. This method provides a reliable technical means for the quantitative characterization of fracture parameters and offers strong support for shale reservoir evaluation and development optimization.",
