        "title": "Suppressing Ghost Artifacts in Pre-Stack Angle Gathers Using a Pre-Established U-Net Trained With Local Stacked Depth Images",
        "doi": "10.1109/tgrs.2025.3647973",
        "authors": "Bingkai Han, Jiankun Jing, Weijian Mao, Zhe Yan, Shaoyong Liu, Guoxin Chen",
        "abstract": "In the marine seismic acquisition, ghost waves are treated as typical noises. They interfere with primary reflections, forming notch frequencies, which decrease the resolution in the data and cause artifacts in the depth migration images. The suppression of ghost waves in the records, as well as the associated artifacts in the images, refers to the necessary seismic processing step named deghosting. Deep-learning-based deghosting methods have been developed in various domains, and they are used as alternatives to conventional deterministic ones for overcoming the drawbacks, e.g., the instability issue in deconvolution-based methods. Usually, for either the data-domain or image-domain deghosting methods, a requisite step is to prepare training sets that are commonly composed of records or images with and without ghost effects, followed by a supervised training process to establish a neural network, e.g., the U-net. Rather than training novel neural networks or tuning pre-trained ones, we explore possibilities to adopt a pre-established deghosting neural network to an extended application scenario. Through a dissection of ghost artifacts in the pre-stack depth image domain, we developed a deghosting approach to directly invoke a pre-established U-net that was already trained with local stacked depth images. We use a Beam pre-stack depth migration engine to generate the ghost-contaminated pre-stack angle-domain image volume. Then, in a nested workflow, we suppress ghost artifacts in the extended angle-domain common image gathers. Through both numerical examples and a field data test, we examine the effectiveness of the proposed deghosting method under various observation geometries, including the conventional towed-streamers and the sparse ocean-bottom nodes. Limitations and potential application prospects are also discussed in depth.",
