        "abstract": "Mining areas are often vegetated and dominated by distributed scatterers (DSs), whose interferometric phases are sensitive to spatiotemporal baselines, causing low coherence and poor phase quality. Phase Linking (PL) has emerged as an effective approach for reconstructing consistent phase of DSs, but most methods assume phase stationarity within the estimation window. In mining scenes, large, rapidly varying subsidence breaks this assumption and causes severe phase bias. In addition, many PL methods underuse polarimetric information and rely on biased sample coherence matrix (SCM) estimates, which further degrade PL accuracy. To address these issues, a mining-oriented neural–polarimetric PL (MNP-PL) approach is proposed, which integrates deep learning with multipolarimetric SAR data to improve accuracy of DS phase estimation in mining areas. A deep neural network is first employed to estimate deformation phases and correct non-stationary interferometric phases in mining areas through defringing processing. Then, polarimetric information from the SCM is fused with spatial features to suppress noise and obtain a more accurate SCM. Finally, adaptive nonlinear shrinkage is applied to the SCM to further improve accuracy of the PL. Simulations show that MNP-PL mitigates the effect of dense fringes and SCM-induced noise, yielding more accurate PL estimates. Real data experiments recover more coherent DS phase time series and clear deformation fringes. Compared with EMI, TP-EMI, MLE-MPPL, and TSO-PL, MNP-PL improves deformation estimation accuracy by 68.02%, 63.40%, 60.36%, and 19.30%, respectively. These results show that MNP-PL is accurate and robust for phase reconstruction in low-coherence, densely fringed mining scenes, enabling precise surface deformation mapping.",
