        "abstract": "Strong reflection masking poses a critical challenge in seismic exploration, especially for deep reservoirs. Although Matching Pursuit (MP)-based methods for strong reflection removal have been extensively studied, their application remains limited by several key issues: inaccurate initial parameter selection, imprecise sparse results, and an inadequate strategy for removing strong-reflection atoms. To address these issues, this study proposes an Iterative Decoupling Matching Pursuit (IDMP) method. The main innovations of IDMP are threefold: (i) Stabilizing initial parameters: using the spectral dominant frequency (replacing error-prone instantaneous attributes) to enhance wavelet initialization precision; (ii) Atomic aliasing identification and decoupling: a designed threshold function identifies aliasing regions, enabling an iterative decoupling process to separate atoms with varying aliasing levels; (iii) A stratigraphic structure adjustment strategy for atomic removal: adjusting the stratigraphic structure based on the geological significance of atoms, thereby ensuring the geological fidelity of the results. Theoretical model experiments confirm IDMP’s superior separation and removal of strong-reflection atoms compared to conventional MP methods, while strictly preserving geological fidelity. In field data, IDMP successfully recovered thin-reservoir reflections masked at 25 ms below a strong interface, which significantly improved the correlation between reservoir thickness and seismic response amplitude, providing a more reliable characterization of reservoir distribution.",
